<h2 id="bg-typ-01">BG-TYP-01: Use the approved typeface, not the official identity font</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to maintained civica interface and documentation typography.

Set civica interface text and documentation in `Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif`. Use regular 400 and bold 700. Do not thin Arial to imitate GDS Transport Light. The supplied civica lockup is artwork, not a font for body text.

GDS Transport is an official GOV.UK identity element, and GOV.UK asks services outside its official domain to use an alternative. Arial is therefore a deliberate choice, not a stand-in for a missing font. Do not download, bundle or silently select GDS Transport from a person’s machine.


<h2 id="bg-typ-02">BG-TYP-02: Build hierarchy with meaning, size and weight</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to headings, labels and explanatory text.

Take page, section and card heading sizes from the [type hierarchy](typography.html). Choose a heading level for the document structure, not for the font size you want. A sensor value is content, not automatically a heading. Use sentence case, readable spacing and bold for selective emphasis.

Left-align instructions in English and Welsh layouts. Long text needs a steady reading edge, so keep centred text for short, deliberate cases. Do not apply a social-media headline length limit to a device name or an error message that needs to wrap.


<h2 id="bg-typ-03">BG-TYP-03: Keep values, units and dates understandable</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to operational readings and exported measurement text.

Show a measurement with its unit and the time it applies to. For example, “Living room 21 °C, updated 14:05”. Use tabular numerals where comparison helps, and keep the text selectable. Never cut off the minus sign, the decimal part, the unit, the entity name or a “stale” or “unavailable” qualifier.

Keep Home Assistant’s language, number, time and timezone preferences for native data. A written report states its period and timezone when the reader could be unsure. Do not bake changing readings into images, and do not set measurements in a display font.


<h2 id="bg-typ-04">BG-TYP-04: Respect native density and operating-system controls</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to custom UI, native dense surfaces, charts and OS-owned controls.

Maintained civica UI uses 19px body text, 16px supporting text and 24px bold card headings, all scaled by Home Assistant’s text preference. Native dense surfaces keep the documented 12, 14, 16 and 19px scale to protect their layout, and native heading-card titles are 24px bold. Chart labels use the adapter’s minimum 14px base.

Do not enlarge every editor row to headline size. Do not restyle the mobile operating system’s share sheet, keyboard or permission dialog, which stay native. Keep monospace in code editors where alignment carries meaning. This is a deliberate Home Assistant adaptation. It does not show that every third-party card follows the type system.


<h2 id="bg-typ-05">BG-TYP-05: Verify text scaling and failure states</h2>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to affected text and controls at narrow widths and user text settings.

Check browser zoom, Home Assistant text scaling and user text-spacing overrides. Let labels, errors and help text grow taller. Test a long room name, a large reading, a missing unit, a translated string and a no-data message. Do not rely on the shortest English demo label.

A tooltip is only a supplement, because keyboard and touch users need the name and essential state without hovering. If a compact native control cuts off non-essential text, keep an accessible full name and a keyboard and touch route to the detail. Do not disable zoom or fix a card’s height to hide overflow.


<h2 id="bg-typ-06">BG-TYP-06: Separate document and promotional typography</h2>

**Local recommendation: SHOULD.** Applies to printable household documents and promotional text.

For a printable household handover, start no smaller than 12pt body text and 16pt headings, as the official print guidance recommends. Use larger text when the reader needs it, and use the household’s own name if it has one. A handover does not need a civica logo. These sizes are a recommendation, not an accessibility guarantee, so inspect the exported pages. Screen CSS does not prove print legibility.

For a release illustration or social image, use a short, readable headline and give the same message as accessible text beside it. Do not replace an HTML guide with a picture of paragraphs. Keep dates, links, measurements and instructions outside the bitmap.


## Review and evidence

The source inventory and rule checklist are in `docs/data/brand-guidance-inventory.json` and `docs/data/brand-guidance-checklist.json`. A reviewed source supports a design decision. It is not a pass for an interface. Before closing a checklist item, record the page or component, the theme and mode, the test result and any remaining issue. Check again after a change to a source, palette, component or renderer.
